<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iyasu V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lij Iyasu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iyasu V, known as Lij Iyasu, was the uncrowned emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> from 1913 to 1916, grandson and designated heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>. His suspected leanings towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> led the nobility and church to depose him in 1916 in favour of Zewditu, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Tafari as regent. He spent years in hiding and captivity, and died in 1935.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iyasu-v.jpg" alt="Iyasu V" width="220" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lidj_Iyasu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
